"value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334114" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "dependent"] ["text" "independent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334114" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "independent"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ (x)^{′} & ({x}^{3}+1)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ 1 & 3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "967d8c3d908e1b439daa76bf7bcf5ea00fae58a3f60a3de205921eb7a79e8fe9"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2{x}^{3}-1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+1"] ["math" "{x}^{3}-x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{3}-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$"] ["text" "zero for all $x\\in R$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "dependent"] ["text" "independent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334120" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "independent"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070787/state.json" "file_sha256" "58861237f091e271e56b4fb081dcd1bbdb2c3b961d89a4d054f0ff1df4188e27"} {"question" "q-334266" "field" nil "attribute" 